course: dictionary
lesson: 13037
title: Fancied: wanted, imagined or expected to win
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Fancied» is the past form of «fancy». It has several meanings, so context helps you pick the right one. If you fancied something, you wanted it or liked the idea of it: «I fancied a cup of tea» means I wanted a cup of tea.
explain: Older books use «fancied» to mean imagined or believed, sometimes wrongly. «She fancied she saw a light in the window» means she thought she saw one, but she may have been mistaken.
explain: In sport, a fancied team or horse is one that people think will win. To check your meaning, swap in «wanted», «imagined» or «expected to win» and see which one makes sense in the sentence.
fact: «Fancy» is very common in UK, Irish, Australian and New Zealand speech for wanting something.
fact: «Fancied» means wanted, or liked the idea of something.
fact: «Fancied» can mean imagined or believed, sometimes wrongly.
fact: In sport, a «fancied» team is one that people expect to win.
fact: A good check is to swap in another word, such as «wanted» or «imagined», and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «I fancied a cookie after school.» What does fancied mean here? | Ran away from | *Wanted | Counted | Dropped
exercise: «He fancied he heard a noise in the attic, but it was only the wind.» What does fancied mean here? | Owned | Painted | Sold | *Imagined, though it was not real
exercise: «The fancied team was expected to win the cup.» Who is the fancied team? | *The team people think will win | The team in red shirts | The team with no players | The team that lost the game
test:
«She fancied a walk in the park on a sunny day.» What does fancied mean here? | Did not like | Bumped into | *Wanted or liked the idea of | Painted
test:
«The old king fancied himself the cleverest man in the land.» What does fancied himself mean? | Wrote a letter to | Forgot about | *Thought he was | Was afraid of
exam:
Which sentence uses fancied to mean wanted? | «She fancied she could fly.» | *«Dad fancied a nap on the sofa.» | «The fancied horse came last.» | «He fancied he saw a ghost.»
